\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Integers are supplied in binary; polynomial time means a polynomial number of classical bit operations in total input length. Given positive integers $g,b,n$ with $n\ge2$, seek a positive integer exponent $x$ satisfying $g^x\equiv b\pmod n$. The computational question is a search problem promised a solution; no coprimality assumption on $g$ is imposed in C22.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is there a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm which produces such an $x$ on every input for which a solution exists? \href{https://mccurley.org/papers/open.ps.gz}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can a modular discrete logarithm be found deterministically in polynomial time for an arbitrary modulus, including composites, whenever it exists?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 8400025 (AMR-083-0025). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Leonard M. Adleman and Kevin S. McCurley, Open Problems in Number Theory Complexity, II, in Algorithmic Number Theory, LNCS 877 (1994), pp. 291--322, C22/O22a, manuscript p. 17 (LNCS p. 307), and Definition 2. \url{https://mccurley.org/papers/open.ps.gz}.
\end{description}
